## 3. Create PostgreSQL Schema

```sql::schema.sql::postgres/init/schema.sql
-- Create payments table
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS payments (
    id VARCHAR(36) PRIMARY KEY,
    amount DECIMAL(19,2) NOT NULL,
    currency VARCHAR(3) NOT NULL,
    status VARCHAR(20) NOT NULL,
    customer_id VARCHAR(255),
    merchant_id VARCHAR(255),
    description VARCHAR(255),
    created_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    updated_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,
    CONSTRAINT chk_status CHECK (status IN ('CANCELLED', 'COMPLETED', 'EXPIRED', 'FAILED', 'PENDING', 'PROCESSING', 'REFUNDED'))
);

-- Create indexes
CREATE INDEX idx_payment_status ON payments(status);
CREATE INDEX idx_payment_created_at ON payments(created_at);
CREATE INDEX idx_payment_customer_id ON payments(customer_id);
```

## 4. Verify Maven POM has PostgreSQL

Ensure your [pom.xml](payment-service/pom.xml) has PostgreSQL dependency instead of MySQL:

```xml::pom.xml::payment-service/pom.xml
<!-- Remove MySQL dependency completely -->
<!-- Add PostgreSQL dependency -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.postgresql</groupId>
    <artifactId>postgresql</artifactId>
    <version>42.7.1</version>
    <scope>runtime</scope>
</dependency>
```

## 5. Clean and Rebuild

Run these commands to clean and rebuild:

```bash
cd payment-service
mvn clean
mvn compile
mvn spring-boot:run
```

## Key Changes Made:

1. **Added missing RabbitMQ properties** in application.yml that were causing the `BeanCreationException`
2. **Ensured PostgreSQL dialect** is properly configured in JPA properties
3. **Updated entity** to use PostgreSQL-compatible types
4. **Created PostgreSQL schema** without MySQL-specific syntax
5. **Set ddl-auto to validate** to prevent Hibernate from trying to create tables with wrong syntax

The main issue was that the RabbitMQ configuration properties were missing from your application.yml file, causing Spring to fail when trying to inject those values into [RabbitMQConfig.java](payment-service/src/main/java/com/example/payment/config/RabbitMQConfig.java).






