52)\n\tat com.mysql.cj.exceptions.ExceptionFactory.createException(ExceptionFactory.java:95)\n\tat com.mysql.cj.exceptions.ExceptionFactory.createException(ExceptionFactory.java:140)\n\tat com.mysql.cj.exceptions.ExceptionFactory.createCommunicationsException(ExceptionFactory.java:156)\n\tat com.mysql.cj.protocol.a.NativeSocketConnection.connect(NativeSocketConnection.java:79)\n\tat com.mysql.cj.NativeSession.connect(NativeSession.java:142)\n\tat com.mysql.cj.jdbc.ConnectionImpl.connectOneTryOnly(ConnectionImpl.java:961)\n\tat com.mysql.cj.jdbc.ConnectionImpl.createNewIO(ConnectionImpl.java:825)\n\t... 58 common frames omitted\nCaused by: java.net.ConnectException: Connection refused\n\tat java.base/sun.nio.ch.Net.pollConnect(Native Method)\n\tat java.base/sun.nio.ch.Net.pollConnectNow(Unknown Source)\n\tat java.base/sun.nio.ch.NioSocketImpl.timedFinishConnect(Unknown Source)\n\tat java.base/sun.nio.ch.NioSocketImpl.connect(Unknown Source)\n\tat java.base/java.net.SocksSocketImpl.connect(Unknown Source)\n\tat java.base/java.net.Socket.connect(Unknown Source)\n\tat com.mysql.cj.protocol.StandardSocketFactory.connect(StandardSocketFactory.java:144)\n\tat com.mysql.cj.protocol.a.NativeSocketConnection.connect(NativeSocketConnection.java:53)\n\t... 61 common frames omitted\n","service":"payment-service","environment":"local"}","selectedFiles":[],"agentId":null,"correlationId":null,"id":"5e39650b-3511-4ec7-a964-d47032de267f","promptLibrary":null,"response":"What we’ll do
- Point the app to the correct DB host inside Docker (use host “mysql”, not “localhost”).
- Remove the deprecated/implicit Hibernate dialect setting to silence WARNs.
- Verify MySQL is healthy, then restart payment-service.

Root cause
- Connection refused means your app tried to connect to the wrong host/port. Inside Docker, jdbc url must use the service name “mysql” (Docker DNS), not localhost. The WARNs about dialect are harmless but can be cleaned up.

Add a docker profile config for the service
- Compose already sets SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=docker for payment-service. Add the following file so the container uses the right JDBC URL and resilient Hikari settings.

```yaml::application-docker.yml::payment-service/src/main/resources/application-docker.yml
spring:
  datasource:
    url: jdbc:mysql://mysql:3306/payment_db?useSSL=false&allowPublicKeyRetrieval=true&serverTimezone=UTC
    username: payment_user
    password: payment_pass
    driver-class-name: com.mysql.cj.jdbc.Driver
    hikari:
      # Don't fail fast if DB is still coming up; pool will retry
      initialization-fail-timeout: 0
      connection-timeout: 30000
      maximum-pool-size: 10

  jpa:
    hibernate:
      ddl-auto: update
    properties:
      hibernate:
        # Do not set 'hibernate.dialect' (Boot/Hibernate will auto-select MySQLDialect)
        format_sql: true
    open-in-view: false

logging:
  level:
    org.hibernate.SQL: warn
```

Clean up deprecated dialect (if set)
- If you set hibernate.dialect in [application.yml](payment-service/src/main/resources/application.yml), remove it (Hibernate auto-detects; the warning you saw is about that).

Verify MySQL and restart
- Ensure your MySQL image tag is valid (recommend mysql:8.4 in [docker-compose.yml](docker/docker-compose.yml)) and the container is healthy.

Check MySQL logs:
```
docker logs -f payment-mysql
```

From payment-service, confirm Docker DNS resolves and port is open:
```
docker exec payment-service getent hosts mysql
```
```
docker exec payment-service /bin/sh -lc "nc -vz mysql 3306 || true"
```

Restart the app after adding application-docker.yml:
```
docker compose up -d --force-recreate payment-service
```

If it still fails
- Ensure MySQL is actually running/healthy:
```
docker ps --filter name=payment-mysql
```
- If you previously tried mysql:9.0 and it initialized a bad data dir, re-init the volume:
```
docker compose down
```
```
docker volume rm docker_mysql_data
```
```
docker compose up -d mysql
```
Then:
```
docker compose up -d payment-service
```

Why this works
- Inside the Docker network, “localhost” refers to the payment-service container itself; using host “mysql” reaches the DB container. Removing the explicit dialect matches Hibernate 6+ behavior and eliminates the deprecation warnings. Hikari’s initialization-fail-timeout avoids failing the app if the DB is still coming up.






